# Exceptions for scripts/verify-refs.mjs.
#
# Every entry is a token the checker would flag but that is not a claim about
# the cap2UI5 repository. Each one needs a reason — an unexplained entry is
# indistinguishable from suppressing a real defect, which is how a checker
# like this stops being worth running.
#
# Format: <token> # <reason>

# --- illustrative paths the READER creates ---------------------------------
srv/app/View1.view.xml  # a static view file the getting-started example tells you to add
app/customer-list       # a hypothetical Fiori elements app, used for contrast

# --- placeholder class names in examples -----------------------------------
z2ui5_cl_app_xyz        # stands for "your app class" in the interface description
my_first_app            # the class the getting-started walkthrough has you write
my_app_name             # stands for "your class" in the ?app_start= URL on the navigation page
zcl_my_app              # stands for "your app class": the name you gave defineApp in the why-cap2UI5 pitch, and the ABAP class on the views page — an ABAP app is registered by its transpiled class, not by defineApp
ClassName               # literal placeholder in the URL-parameter description
z2ui5_cl_smp_app_493    # abap2UI5's hello-world sample: a class in abap2UI5/samples and @cap2ui5/samples, not in abap2UI5's src

# --- apps of other repositories -------------------------------------------
# Registered with defineApp in their own repository, not in cap2UI5 - and apps,
# not framework classes, whatever their z2ui5_ prefix says to the class check.
z2ui5_cl_caps_app_001   # cap2UI5/samples-stack's OData sample, linked from examples/external-odata
z2ui5_cl_caps_app_002   # cap2UI5/samples-stack's RFC sample, linked from examples/external-odata

# Upstream's frozen src/99 needs no entries any more: the plugin hosts
# upstream's whole transpiled runtime, so z2ui5_cl_xml_view and its siblings
# ARE in it (measured in the assembled output) and resolve like any other
# class. They are still not what to write new code against — see
# guide/vs-abap2ui5 — but "do not use this" is a recommendation, and this file
# is only about what exists.

# --- plugin options without a default in the plugin's package.json ---------
# verify-refs reads the option names from package.json#cds.requires.cap2ui5,
# which holds apps, roles and routes. These two are read by
# plugin/lib/config.js, which holds their defaults (60000 ms; off).
cap2ui5.timeout         # the ms an async main( ) may take — config.js timeoutOf( ); since 0.6.0
cap2ui5.agent           # the MCP endpoint's switch and settings — config.js / lib/agent/policy.js agentConfig( ), since 0.5.0

# --- release numbers that are NOT the pinned runtime release ----------------
# The pin itself (whatever cap2UI5's main pins) is read from the checkout
# and never listed here — listing it would keep the old number green after a
# pin move, which is the one defect the release check exists to catch.
#
# The two below WERE pins. They are listed because the site now says which
# plugin release pinned which runtime (reference/configuration, "The
# runtime"), and that stays true whatever main pins. The price: a page that
# still called one of them the current pin would pass — so name them only
# together with the plugin version they belong to.
1.146.0                 # the runtime plugin 0.4.0 pinned — history, in the plugin-to-runtime table
1.147.0                 # the runtime plugin 0.5.0 pinned — history, in the plugin-to-runtime table
1.143.0                 # the upstream release where _bind/_bind_edit merged — history, stays true whatever the pin says
1.113.0                 # an OpenUI5 release — a UI5 number, not a framework release
